{
  "image_name": "2aff96da428ca92c586cb828b4f1bb15.png",
  "prompt_text": "This figure summarizes how short-chain fatty acids (SCFAs), likely acetate, propionate, and butyrate, act on colon cells at the gut lining. At the apical membrane, SCFAs are transported into colonocytes through labeled carriers and also signal through membrane receptors such as FFAR2 and GPR109A. In the lumen, their presence appears to lower pH, which is linked to reduced pathogens and increased free calcium, supporting colonization resistance and mineral absorption.\n\nInside the colonocyte, SCFAs have both metabolic and gene-regulatory roles. One pathway shows mitochondrial beta-oxidation producing acetyl-CoA and ATP, emphasizing their use as an energy source. Another centers on the nucleus, where SCFAs influence gene expression through epigenetic mechanisms: histone acetylation, inhibition of HDACs, and effects on DNA methylation machinery such as DNMT1. The diagram also indicates regulation through miRNAs that affect mRNA translation at the ribosome.\n\nThese combined actions are tied to many intracellular outcomes, including energy provision, gut motility, barrier maintenance, immunity and inflammation, cell proliferation/apoptosis, oxidative stress, and mineral absorption. The figure also suggests broader systemic effects beyond the gut, including impacts on metabolism, cardiovascular function, and the brain.",
